---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Sets"
  description="Compare grow-only, two-phase, and observed-remove replicated sets."
  territory="Structures"
  sheetTitle="Sets"
>
  <article class="set-family">
    <header class="page-intro">
      <p class="territory-label">Structure family</p>
      <h1>Sets</h1>
      <p>
        A ranger asks Alice, Bob, and Carol to survey trail beacons before the
        next hiking season. Each hiker takes a separate route and writes active
        beacon names in a field notebook. They exchange copies when radio or
        trail relays permit, and the ranger needs their notebooks to converge
        on one survey list.
      </p>
      <p>
        The ranger does not assign sequence numbers to these reports. Each set
        carries enough evidence to merge delayed, repeated, or reordered
        notebook copies.
      </p>
    </header>

    <section aria-labelledby="g-set-title">
      <header>
        <div>
          <p class="structure-kind">CRDT</p>
          <h2 id="g-set-title">GSet</h2>
          <p>An add-only set that merges by union.</p>
        </div>
        <code>g_set_kernel</code>
      </header>
      <div class="structure-body">
        <div>
          <p>
            The survey starts when Alice finds the Eagle Creek beacon and adds
            its name. Bob and Carol are on other trails, so they may receive
            her report late or more than once. A GSet handles that uncertainty
            with one rule: each hiker can add a name, and merge takes the
            union.
          </p>
          <p>
            Soon Alice returns to Eagle Creek and finds that the old beacon has
            been taken down. The ranger now needs a list of active beacons, not
            a permanent record of every beacon ever seen. A GSet cannot record
            Alice's removal.
          </p>
          <h3>Best for</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Recorded event IDs, observed devices, and permanent labels</li>
            <li>Membership that must only grow</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Updates</dt><dd>Add only</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Merge rule</dt><dd>Set union</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Metadata</dt><dd>One retained entry per member</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <a class="structure-link" href="/structures/g-set/">
        Open the GSet lesson
      </a>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="two-p-set-title">
      <header>
        <div>
          <p class="structure-kind">CRDT</p>
          <h2 id="two-p-set-title">TwoPSet</h2>
          <p>A removable set where retirement is permanent.</p>
        </div>
        <code>two_p_set_kernel</code>
      </header>
      <div class="structure-body">
        <div>
          <p>
            The hikers add a second grow-only set for retirements. Alice places
            <code>Eagle Creek</code> in it. Her notebook now contains the name
            in both its additions and removals, so her visible list is empty.
            Bob and Carol may still show Eagle Creek until Alice's removal
            record reaches them.
          </p>
          <p>
            The next morning, Bob installs a replacement beacon at the same
            place. Its list name is still <code>Eagle Creek</code>. The old
            tombstone blocks his new add because a TwoPSet cannot distinguish
            the replacement from the retired installation.
          </p>
          <h3>Best for</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Revoked credentials and decommissioned equipment</li>
            <li>Membership where removal must win permanently</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Updates</dt><dd>Add once, remove once</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Merge rule</dt><dd>Union additions and removals</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Metadata</dt><dd>Members plus permanent tombstones</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <a class="structure-link" href="/structures/two-p-set/">
        Open the TwoPSet lesson
      </a>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="or-set-title">
      <header>
        <div>
          <p class="structure-kind">CRDT</p>
          <h2 id="or-set-title">Observed-remove set</h2>
          <p>A removable set that permits a later or concurrent re-add.</p>
        </div>
        <code>or_set_kernel</code>
      </header>
      <div class="structure-body">
        <div>
          <p>
            The final design gives every installation a unique tag. Alice's
            notebook marks the old tag <code>A:1</code> as removed. Bob's
            notebook records the replacement under fresh tag
            <code>B:2</code>. Carol still has only <code>A:1</code> until the
            new records arrive. She can receive them in either order: the old
            installation stays removed, and the replacement remains.
          </p>
          <p>
            The ranger receives the same converged list without assigning an
            order to the hikers' updates. The tags contain the evidence needed
            for merge.
          </p>
          <h3>Best for</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Collaborative selections, tags, rosters, and carts</li>
            <li>Membership that must support remove and re-add</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <dl>
          <div><dt>Updates</dt><dd>Add, remove, and re-add</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Merge rule</dt><dd>Union tags, then subtract observed removals</dd></div>
          <div><dt>Metadata</dt><dd>Live addition tags plus removal knowledge</dd></div>
        </dl>
      </div>
      <a class="structure-link" href="/structures/observed-remove-set/">
        Open the observed-remove set lesson
      </a>
    </section>

    <section class="family-comparison" aria-labelledby="set-choice-title">
      <h2 id="set-choice-title">Removal determines the set</h2>
      <table aria-label="Replicated set comparison">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Need</th>
            <th scope="col">Choose</th>
            <th scope="col">Cost</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">No item removal</th>
            <td>GSet</td>
            <td>Retain each member</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Removal is permanent</th>
            <td>TwoPSet</td>
            <td>Retain every removed value</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">Items can return</th>
            <td>Observed-remove set</td>
            <td>Retain identities and removal knowledge</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </section>

    <nav class="structure-next" aria-label="Continue through structures">
      <a href="/structures/counters/">Back to counters</a>
      <a href="/structures/g-set/">Start the set lessons</a>
      <a href="/atlas/multi-value-registers/">Continue to registers</a>
    </nav>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .set-family {
    max-width: var(--measure-reading);
    margin-inline: auto;
  }

  .page-intro {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-7);
  }

  .page-intro > p:last-child {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
  }

  .set-family > section:not(.family-comparison) {
    position: relative;
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    padding: var(--space-5);
    border: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    background: var(--clear);
  }

  section > header {
    display: flex;
    align-items: start;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-5);
    padding-bottom: var(--space-4);
    border-bottom: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
  }

  section h2,
  section header p {
    margin: 0;
  }

  section h2 {
    margin-block: var(--space-1);
  }

  .structure-kind {
    color: var(--instrument);
    font-family: var(--font-data);
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  section header code {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    white-space: nowrap;
  }

  .structure-body {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1.3fr) minmax(15rem, 0.7fr);
    gap: var(--space-6);
    padding-block: var(--space-5);
  }

  .structure-body p,
  .structure-body h3 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .structure-body h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-2);
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .structure-body ul {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  dl {
    margin: 0;
    border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
  }

  dl div {
    padding-block: var(--space-3);
  }

  dl div + div {
    border-top: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line-faint);
  }

  dt {
    color: var(--muted);
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  dd {
    margin: var(--space-1) 0 0;
  }

  .structure-link {
    display: inline-block;
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-weight: 700;
  }

  .family-comparison {
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
  }

  table {
    width: 100%;
    margin-block: var(--space-4);
    border-collapse: collapse;
  }

  :is(th, td) {
    padding: var(--space-3);
    border-block: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    text-align: left;
    vertical-align: top;
  }

  th {
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
  }

  .structure-next {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: var(--space-4);
    margin-top: var(--space-7);
    padding-block: var(--space-5);
    border-top: var(--rule-width) solid var(--line);
    font-family: var(--font-ui);
    font-weight: 650;
  }

  @media (width < 42rem) {
    .set-family > section:not(.family-comparison) {
      padding: var(--space-4);
    }

    section > header,
    .structure-body {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }

    section > header {
      display: grid;
      gap: var(--space-2);
    }

    .family-comparison {
      overflow-x: auto;
    }
  }
</style>
